# CLI Command Attribute

This document describes the `[CliCommand]` attribute used to mark methods as CLI commands.

**Location:** `Attributes/CliCommandAttribute.cs`

```csharp
namespace Cyrena.CLI.Attributes;

[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Method, Inherited = false, AllowMultiple = false)]
public sealed class CliCommandAttribute : Attribute
{
    public CliCommandAttribute(string name);

    public string Name { get; }
    public string? Description { get; set; }
}
```

## Purpose

The `CliCommandAttribute` marks a method as a CLI command that can be invoked from the command line. Methods must be:
- Inside a class marked with `[CliSurface]`
- Public
- Marked with `[CliCommand]`

## Properties

### Name
The command name used to invoke the method from the command line.

```csharp
[CliCommand("configure")]  // Invoked as: ./app configure
public void Configure() { }
```

### Description
Optional description shown in help text.

```csharp
[CliCommand("configure", Description = "Configures application settings")]
public void Configure() { }
```

## Usage

### Basic Command

```csharp
[CliSurface]
public class ConfigCommands
{
    [CliCommand("set")]
    public void SetConfig(
        [CliParam("key")] string key,
        [CliParam("value")] string value)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Set {key}={value}");
    }
}
```

**Invocation:**
```bash
./app set --key=timeout --value=30
```

### Command with Description

```csharp
[CliSurface]
public class DataCommands
{
    [CliCommand("migrate", Description = "Runs database migrations")]
    public async Task Migrate()
    {
        await RunMigrationsAsync();
    }
}
```

**Help Output:**
```
Available Commands:
  migrate - Runs database migrations
```

### Command with Return Value

```csharp
[CliSurface]
public class ValidateCommands
{
    [CliCommand("validate")]
    public CliExecutionResult Validate()
    {
        if (!IsValid())
            return CliExecutionResult.Stop(1, "Validation failed");

        return CliExecutionResult.CommandExecuted("OK!");
    }
}
```

### Async Command

```csharp
[CliSurface]
public class BackupCommands
{
    [CliCommand("backup")]
    public async Task<CliExecutionResult> Backup(
        [CliParam("path")] string path)
    {
        try
        {
            await CreateBackupAsync(path);
            return CliExecutionResult.CommandExecuted("Backup created!");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            return CliExecutionResult.Stop(1, ex.Message);
        }
    }
}
```

## Method Signature Requirements

| Requirement | Details |
|-------------|---------|
| **Visibility** | Must be `public` |
| **Containing Class** | Must be in a `[CliSurface]` class |
| **Return Type** | `void`, `CliExecutionResult`, `Task`, or `Task<CliExecutionResult>` |
| **Parameters** | Must be marked with `[CliParam]` to be bound from CLI |
| **Static/Instance** | Both supported |

### Valid Signatures

```csharp
[CliSurface]
public class ValidCommands
{
    // Void method
    [CliCommand("cmd1")]
    public void Command1() { }

    // With parameters
    [CliCommand("cmd2")]
    public void Command2([CliParam("x")] int x) { }

    // Returns CliExecutionResult
    [CliCommand("cmd3")]
    public CliExecutionResult Command3() => CliExecutionResult.Continue();

    // Async void
    [CliCommand("cmd4")]
    public async Task Command4() { await Task.Delay(0); }

    // Async with result
    [CliCommand("cmd5")]
    public async Task<CliExecutionResult> Command5() 
    {
        await Task.Delay(0);
        return CliExecutionResult.CommandExecuted();
    }

    // Static method
    [CliCommand("cmd6")]
    public static void Command6() { }
}
```

### Invalid Signatures

```csharp
[CliSurface]
public class InvalidCommands
{
    // Missing [CliCommand] - not registered
    public void NotACommand() { }

    // Private method - not registered
    [CliCommand("private")]
    private void PrivateCommand() { }

    // Returns unsupported type - registered but return value ignored
    [CliCommand("returns-int")]
    public int ReturnsInt() => 42;  // Works, but return value ignored
}
```

## Command Name Conventions

- Use lowercase with hyphens: `set-config`, `run-migration`
- Be descriptive but concise
- Avoid abbreviations unless well-known
- Use verbs for actions: `create`, `delete`, `update`, `list`

### Good Examples
```csharp
[CliCommand("create-user")]
[CliCommand("list-configs")]
[CliCommand("reset-database")]
```

### Poor Examples
```csharp
[CliCommand("cu")]           // Too abbreviated
[CliCommand("doTheThing")]   // CamelCase, vague
[CliCommand("USER_CREATE")]  // Wrong casing convention
```

## Complete Example

```csharp
using Cyrena.CLI.Attributes;
using Cyrena.CLI.Models;

namespace MyApp.CLI;

[CliSurface(Description = "Application management commands")]
public class AppCommands
{
    private readonly IAppService _app;

    public AppCommands(IAppService app)
    {
        _app = app;
    }

    [CliCommand("start", Description = "Starts the application")]
    public void Start()
    {
        _app.Start();
        Console.WriteLine("Application started.");
    }

    [CliCommand("stop", Description = "Stops the application")]
    public async Task<CliExecutionResult> Stop(
        [CliParam("force", DefaultValue = false, Description = "Force stop")] bool force)
    {
        try
        {
            await _app.StopAsync(force);
            return CliExecutionResult.CommandExecuted("Application stopped.");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            return CliExecutionResult.Stop(1, $"Failed to stop: {ex.Message}");
        }
    }

    [CliCommand("status", Description = "Shows application status")]
    public void Status()
    {
        var status = _app.GetStatus();
        Console.WriteLine($"Status: {status}");
    }
}
```

**Help Output:**
```
Available Commands:
  start - Starts the application
  status - Shows application status
  stop - Stops the application

Use '--help <command>' for more information on a specific command.
```

**Detailed Help:**
```bash
./app help stop
```
```
Usage: stop [options]

Description:
  Stops the application

Options:
  --force<bool> [default: False]
      Force stop
```
